## One major advantage over Tantra

You can get meaningful expert feedback much sooner.

The early Buddhist source corpus and interpretive debates are considerably more accessible, and Ñāṇavīra's corpus is explicitly published online in structured pages alongside *Clearing the Path*. ([Ñāṇavīra.org][4])

So you could prototype this without hunting obscure manuscripts.

It's a very good way to test whether your system handles:

```text
same base corpus
+ competing translations
+ competing interpretations
+ philosophical arguments
```

whereas the Greek pilot tests:

```text
fragments
+ lost works
+ transmission
+ argument dependency
```

and Tantra tests:

```text
poorly translated texts
+ specialist terminology
+ manuscript/source complexity
```

Those are three powerful stress tests of the same underlying engine.

## I think the larger architecture may actually be this

You're starting to uncover a more general thing:

```text
AUDITABLE TEXTUAL TRADITIONS ENGINE
│
├── Tantra
│   translation + terminology + manuscripts
│
├── Early Buddhism
│   Pāli + interpretive disagreement
│
└── Late Platonism
    Greek + arguments + fragments
```

Same primitives:

```text
Identity
Assertion
Evidence
Provenance
Review
Rights
```

but different domain modules.

I would **not** split your attention three ways yet. But as a proof that the architecture generalizes, early Buddhism is an excellent candidate.

My first Buddhist pilot would actually be:

> **Ñāṇananda + Ñāṇavīra on dependent arising, anchored to ~10–15 core suttas.**

Not “translate Buddhism.”

Build a page that lets someone answer:

> **What exactly is dependent arising, and where do these serious interpreters disagree about it?**

If that page is excellent, you've got something genuinely unusual.

